}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186508990 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186509457 1071 "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $a+(4b-4c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186509457 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $a+(4b-4c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186509465 1071 "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $3a+(2b-(-5)c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186509465 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $3a+(2b-(-5)c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186509527 1071 "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $(-3)a-((-3)b+3c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186509527 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ Find $(-3)a-((-3)b+3c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186509551 1071 "Let $$\n\\displaystyle a=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle c=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Find $5a-(b+5c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186509551 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, $b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ and $c=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find $5a-(b+5c).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186510364 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186510364 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186510371 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.\n$$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186510371 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  &