$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(4,1)$ to $(6,2).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186519977 1071 "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(12,5).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186519977 1071 "Evaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}8 y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y + 2$ from $(6,2)$ to $(12,5).$" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186519979 1071 "![](images/ad/ad80637c8c61ee847bd385c39fb0eec5fc1b14ee4f9d6c1796fb31baff48c016.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 \\sqrt{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(2 \\sqrt{5},5),$ as shown above." 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186519979 1071 "![](images/ad/ad80637c8c61ee847bd385c39fb0eec5fc1b14ee4f9d6c1796fb31baff48c016.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}8 x y\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 \\sqrt{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(2 \\sqrt{5},5),$ as shown above." 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186520472 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186520472 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186520496 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=(z-2)^{2},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139539104 17592186576411 true] [17592186520496 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=(z-2)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139539104 17592186576411 false] [17592186520514 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $$\n\\d